---
/**
 * The front door: what the site offers, what $10,000 did in each portfolio, four cards,
 * which one is for you, and how it was tested.
 *
 * Every number on this page is read from the series the research emitter writes to
 * `src/content/series/` and formatted here; none is typed.
 */
import Button from "~/components/Button.astro";
import GrowthChart from "~/components/GrowthChart.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import PortfolioCard from "~/components/PortfolioCard.astro";
import { PORTFOLIOS, type Portfolio } from "~/content/portfolios";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { formatDollars, formatMonths, formatPct } from "~/lib/charts";
import { loadSeries, seriesFor } from "~/lib/series";

const file = loadSeries();
const window = file.window.label;
const market = seriesFor("market");
const lines = [market, ...PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => seriesFor(p.slug))];

const cards = PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => ({ ...p, summary: seriesFor(p.slug).summary }));
const byId = (slug: Portfolio["slug"]) => {
  const found = cards.find((c) => c.slug === slug);
  if (found === undefined) throw new Error(`No card for ${slug}`);
  return found;
};
const oneFund = byId("one-fund");
const valueLean = byId("value-lean");
const withTrend = byId("with-trend");
const cautious = byId("cautious");

/** How sure, and of what, in the reader's words. The word itself is in `portfolios.ts`. */
const GLOSS: Readonly<Record<Portfolio["slug"], string>> = {
  "one-fund": "Broad world stock exposure at a low stated fee.",
  "value-lean": "That it beats a plain world index by a little, over decades.",
  "with-trend": "Whether it beats a plain world index.",
  cautious: "Whether its trend part earns its fee. Less stock exposure does not guarantee a smaller loss.",
};

const year = (month: string) => month.slice(0, 4);
const fallOf = (c: (typeof cards)[number]) =>
  `${formatDollars(c.summary.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough)} (${formatPct(c.summary.worstFall.pct)})`;

const cautiousLong = seriesFor("cautious", "1929").summary.worstFall;
---

<Base title="Kelly Portfolios" bareTitle xl>
  <section class="wide">
    <Hero
      title="Four portfolios you can actually buy, tested as whole portfolios."
      lede="Compare the holdings, fees and simulated histories. Choose the risks that fit your plans; these are starting points, not a ranking of guaranteed winners."
    >
      <p class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <Button href="/portfolios/">See the four portfolios</Button>
        <Button href="#choose" variant="outline">How to choose</Button>
      </p>
    </Hero>
  </section>

  <div class="wide mt-10">
    <GrowthChart series={lines} title="What $10,000 became">
      Four portfolios and a plain world stock index (65% US stocks, 35% the rest of the world), {window}; each
      gridline is a bigger step than the last. Simulated from index data, not fund records: the trend part is an
      index before trading costs, and its fund has existed only since September 2023. One fund and the plain index
      are the same mix on this history, so their lines overlap.
    </GrowthChart>
  </div>

  <div class="card-grid-4 wide mt-10">
    {
      cards.map((p) => (
        <PortfolioCard
          number={p.number}
          name={p.name}
          href={p.href}
          tagline={p.tagline}
          holdings={p.holdings}
          became={formatDollars(p.summary.final)}
          fell={formatDollars(p.summary.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough)}
          fellPct={formatPct(p.summary.worstFall.pct)}
          costs={p.feeOn10k}
          period={`${window}, simulated`}
          confidence={p.confidence}
          gloss={GLOSS[p.slug]}
        />
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14" id="choose">
    <h2>Which one is for you</h2>

    <p>
      Start with when you need the money, the losses you could afford, and the risks you are willing to take.
      Keep money needed soon separate from a long-term stock allocation. The losses below illustrate the
      simulated histories; they are not limits on future losses. Each is on $10,000 held at the top,{" "}
      {window}.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        If an all-stock allocation fits your horizon and you want the simplest option, consider{" "}
        <a href={oneFund.href}>{oneFund.name}</a>. Its worst fall took $10,000 down to {fallOf(oneFund)} between{" "}
        {year(oneFund.summary.worstFall.peak)} and {year(oneFund.summary.worstFall.trough)}; it was back above
        $10,000 after {formatMonths(oneFund.summary.worstFall.monthsToRecover)}.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you accept stock-market risk and long periods behind an index in pursuit of a value advantage, consider <a href={valueLean.href}>{valueLean.name}</a>. Its worst fall took $10,000 down to{" "}
        {fallOf(valueLean)}, back above $10,000 after {formatMonths(valueLean.summary.worstFall.monthsToRecover)}.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you want trend alongside stocks and accept its costs, extra market exposure and uncertain contribution, consider{" "}
        <a href={withTrend.href}>{withTrend.name}</a>. Its worst fall took $10,000 down to {fallOf(withTrend)}, back
        above $10,000 after {formatMonths(withTrend.summary.worstFall.monthsToRecover)}.
      </li>
      <li>
        For less stock exposure, consider <a href={cautious.href}>{cautious.name}</a>. Its worst
        fall took $10,000 down to {fallOf(cautious)}, back above $10,000 after{" "}
        {formatMonths(cautious.summary.worstFall.monthsToRecover)}. It can still lose much more: on the long simulated history, across{" "}
        {year(cautiousLong.peak)} to {year(cautiousLong.trough)}, the same mix fell{" "}
        {formatPct(Math.abs(cautiousLong.pct))}.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      One fund is the simple stock-market starting point. It does not decide how much of your money belongs in
      stocks. The <a href="/how-to-hold/">holding guide</a> covers cash needs and accounts;
      <a href="/strategies/stacking/">stacking strategies</a> explains what an extra fund needs to contribute.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>How this was tested</h2>

    <p>
      The research compares whole portfolios against explicit alternatives. Some ideas have only exploratory
      evidence; others have not yet been measured. The portfolios are simulated from index data, not fund records, and
      every dollar figure is before tax. Each result carries one of five words, <a href="/about/">Settled</a>,{" "}
      <a href="/about/">Probably</a>, <a href="/about/">Too close to call</a>, <a href="/about/">No</a> or{" "}
      <a href="/about/">Not measured yet</a>; the <a href="/about/">About page</a> says what each one means and how
      the testing works.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
